<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<xs:schema
  xmlns:xs="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema"
  xmlns="http://example.org/schema/drug/1.0"
  targetNamespace="http://example.org/schema/drug/1.0"
  elementFormDefault="qualified"
  version="1.0">

  <xs:annotation>
    <xs:documentation>
      This is an example XML Schema which provides a minimal description of a drug: name, dm+d VTM code,
      units of measure and acceptable routes (SNOMED CT coded). It is provided as a brief overview of how a schema might be built,
      how it can be documented and that documentation displayed for easier human understanding.

      This schema is a example and will *not* be used in the final build, although something similar may be required!
    </xs:documentation>
  </xs:annotation>

  <!-- SNOMED CT identifier: 6 to 18 digits, no leading zero -->
  <xs:simpleType name="SctId">
    <xs:restriction base="xs:string">
      <xs:pattern value="[1-9][0-9]{5,17}"/>
    </xs:restriction>
    <xs:documentation>
      Where a data item is a numerical identifier, then we can define details of that such as in this case where a SNOMED CT code might be used. While the scheme does not check that the correct code has been used, it can provide some validation that a potentially valid code has been used, here it will allow a number with no trailing zero (shown by [1-9]), followed by further digits which obviously can have a zero [0-9] and the SNOMED code can be 6 to 18 characters long, so the remaining digits are repeated between {5 and 17} times.
    </xs:documentation>
  </xs:simpleType>

  <!-- A SNOMED CT concept: code plus human-readable term -->
  <xs:complexType name="CodedConcept">
    <xs:simpleContent>
      <xs:extension base="xs:string">
        <xs:attribute name="code" type="SctId" use="required"/>
      </xs:extension>
    <xs:documentation>
Sometimes it will be easier for a data element to contain both a SNOMED CT code and its accompaying text description. In this case we can apply the same restrictions for a valid code described above, but set them as an attribute as part of a string coded concept.  
    </xs:documentation>
    </xs:simpleContent>
  </xs:complexType>

  <xs:element name="drug">
    <xs:complexType>
      <xs:sequence>
        <xs:element name="name" type="xs:string"/>
        <xs:element name="vtm" type="CodedConcept">
            <xs:documentation>dm+d Virtual Therapeutic Moiety. The text is the VTM name (e.g. Aspirin), with an accompanying coded vtm value. In this case no restrictions on code lengths has been set</xs:documentation>
        </xs:element>
        <xs:element name="unitsOfMeasure">
          <xs:complexType>
            <xs:sequence>
              <xs:element name="unit" type="CodedConcept" maxOccurs="unbounded"/>
            <xs:documentation>Units of measure can be coded from SNOMED CT.  In this example, multiple units of measure are allowedm (maxOccurs). In a chemotherapy protocol, it is likely that only one unit of measure will be permitted per drug.</xs:documentation>
            </xs:sequence>
          </xs:complexType>
        </xs:element>
        <xs:element name="routes">
          <xs:complexType>
            <xs:sequence>
              <xs:element name="route" type="CodedConcept" maxOccurs="unbounded"/>
            <xs:documentation>Routes of administration can be coded from SNOMED CT. Multiple routes of administration for a drug would be permitted. </xs:documentation>
            </xs:sequence>
          </xs:complexType>
        </xs:element>
      </xs:sequence>
    </xs:complexType>
  </xs:element>
</xs:schema>
